---
import BlogArticleShell from '../../../components/BlogArticleShell.astro';
import BlogArticleInlineFigures from '../../../components/BlogArticleInlineFigures.astro';

export const prerender = true;

const canonicalPath = '/blog/qc-retake-reject-decision-spreadsheet-2026';
const publishedISO = '2026-06-29';
---

<BlogArticleShell
  title="QC retake vs reject: spreadsheet buyer decision guide (2026) | kakobuy-cn.com"
  description="When warehouse photos fail your standards—retake, exchange, or reject? A 2026 editorial framework for Kakobuy Spreadsheet shoppers balancing time, seller policy, and batch quality."
  canonicalPath={canonicalPath}
  headline="QC retake vs reject: how to decide when spreadsheet rows fail warehouse photos (2026)"
  publishedISO={publishedISO}
  displayDate="29 June 2026"
  structuredKeywords={[
    'Kakobuy QC retake reject',
    'warehouse photo decision 2026',
    'spreadsheet QC exchange',
    'agent retake policy reps',
    'kakobuy-cn.com QC guide',
  ]}
>
  <p class="blog-lede" slot="lede">
    Warehouse QC is the moment spreadsheet optimism meets factory variance—stitching off, colour shifted, logo crooked—and most buyers freeze between “retake” and “reject.”
    This guide is editorial context for <strong>2026</strong> decisions on the <strong><a href="/">Kakobuy Spreadsheet</a></strong> workflow: when a retake is worth the wait, when rejection saves money, and when community batch lore should not override your eyes.
  </p>

  <h2>Three outcomes, one clock</h2>
    <BlogArticleInlineFigures slug="qc-retake-reject-decision-spreadsheet-2026" />

  <div class="blog-table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Action</th><th scope="col">Best when</th><th scope="col">Risk</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Approve</strong></td><td>Flaws within your personal GL standard</td><td>You own the item; no second chance</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Retake photos</strong></td><td>Lighting/angle hides detail; item may be fine</td><td>Delays storage window; not a new unit</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Reject / exchange</strong></td><td>Clear RL flaw or wrong variant shipped</td><td>Seller may refuse; domestic return fees</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Kakobuy policy details live in their signed-in UI—we explain buyer psychology, not agent legal terms.
  </p>

  <h2>Retake triggers that make sense</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Glare on patent leather or hardware</strong>—macro retake before you panic.</li>
    <li><strong>Single-angle stills on curved panels</strong>—bags, toe boxes, blazer shoulders.</li>
    <li><strong>Size tag not visible</strong>—request flat lay with label in frame.</li>
    <li><strong>Video QC unavailable</strong>—ask for short clip—<a href="/blog/kakobuy-4k-qc-video-inspection-guide/">4K video guide</a>.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Retakes are cheap in ¥ but expensive in days—factor <a href="/blog/kakobuy-warehouse-storage-spreadsheet/">storage limits</a> if your cart is near expiry.
  </p>

  <h2>Reject triggers that save money</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Wrong colour or size</strong> vs your order—see <a href="/blog/spreadsheet-wrong-variant-recovery-2026/">variant recovery guide</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Obvious batch downgrade</strong>—logo font, sole shape, lining colour.</li>
    <li><strong>Structural defects</strong>—asymmetric toe, collapsed heel, torn lining.</li>
    <li><strong>Repeated retake still hides flaw</strong>—trust the pattern, not hope.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Community QC helps research—<a href="/blog/community-crowdsourced-qc-verification-batches/">crowdsourced verification</a>—but warehouse photos are authoritative for <em>your</em> unit.
  </p>

  <h2>Category-specific thresholds</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Sneakers:</strong> toe box and heel cup—<a href="/blog/kakobuy-sneakers-spreadsheet-lane-guide-2026/">sneakers lane</a>.
    <strong>Bags:</strong> monogram alignment—<a href="/blog/kakobuy-bags-spreadsheet-lane-guide-2026/">bags guide</a>.
    <strong>Apparel:</strong> measurement drift—<a href="/blog/kakobuy-sizing-spreadsheet-apparel/">sizing guide</a>.
    Higher ¥ tiers deserve stricter thresholds; budget rows need realistic GL expectations.
  </p>

  <h2>Decision workflow</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Compare warehouse stills to listing + spreadsheet row on <strong><a href="/">Kakobuy Spreadsheet home</a></strong>.</li>
    <li>Request retake if information is missing—not if flaw is confirmed.</li>
    <li>Reject early if variant is wrong; delay burns storage.</li>
    <li>Document decision screenshots for disputes.</li>
    <li>Re-order from trusted seller URL if batch is systemic—<a href="/blog/spotting-red-flag-sellers-community-spreadsheets/">red flag audit</a>.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Full QC discipline: <a href="/blog/kakobuy-spreadsheet-qc/">warehouse QC guide</a>.
    Second haul buyers should tighten standards—<a href="/blog/kakobuy-second-haul-spreadsheet-strategy-2026/">second haul playbook</a>.
  </p>
</BlogArticleShell>
